  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-167105",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB},$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nm=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $m=-2$ that passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,0).$ \n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-2\\cdot (x-4) \\\\ y & =-2x+8\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
